;; FH-ROUTELINK-008 — `:prefetch :intent` binds the three intent
;; positions, and binds nothing else.
;;
;; The whole value of a prefetch is that it fires BEFORE the user commits,
;; and the whole risk is that it fires when they never intended to commit
;; at all. So the law has two halves and the second is the one worth
;; testing: an opted-in link warms its destination on hover, focus and
;; touch — and a link that did not opt in binds nothing, a render
;; dispatches nothing, and `:prefetch` never reaches the `<a>` as an
;; attribute (`prefetch="intent"` is not HTML).
;;
;; The third claim is composition. An opted-in link takes the three
;; positions over, so a caller's own hover handler would be the natural
;; casualty: it must still run, and it must run FIRST. Because the
;; framework CALLS it, the accepted grammar at those positions narrows to
;; the same closed roster `:on-click` carries — and only on a link that
;; opted in, which is why the passive link below carries an event VECTOR
;; at the same key and renders happily.
;;
;; The address is the link's OWN: same `:to` and `:params` as the href, so
;; a warm-up can never fetch a different destination than the click will
;; navigate to. `:fragment` is absent by construction — a `#fragment` is
;; never a resource input.
{:fh/id "FH-ROUTELINK-008"
 :fh/law
 "v/route-link's :prefetch is a routing control key stripped before DOM emission; :prefetch :intent binds :on-mouse-enter / :on-focus / :on-touch-start to the routing prefetch for the link's own address — composing with a caller handler of the same name rather than replacing it, and narrowing those positions to the same closed callback roster :on-click carries — while a link that did not opt in binds none of them and a render dispatches nothing."

 :routes
 {:fh.route/cart    "/cart"
  :fh.route/article "/articles/:slug"}

 ;; The opted-in link, the href it renders, and the event its intent
 ;; positions dispatch. The address is selected from the link's own props,
 ;; so the two agree by construction rather than by discipline.
 :opt-in
 {:props {:to :fh.route/article :params {:slug "the-intro"}
          :query {:tab "notes"} :prefetch :intent :class "title"}
  :href  "/articles/the-intro?tab=notes"
  :event [:rf.route/prefetch {:to :fh.route/article
                              :params {:slug "the-intro"}
                              :query  {:tab "notes"}}]}

 ;; The three positions the opt-in makes framework-owned, in the order the
 ;; spec names them: pointer hover, focus, touch-start.
 :intent-keys [:on-mouse-enter :on-focus :on-touch-start]

 ;; `:prefetch` is a routing control key and never an HTML attribute.
 :control-key :prefetch

 ;; The passive link: same route, no `:prefetch`. Nothing is bound, so its
 ;; intent positions stay ordinary OPEN Freehand event positions — which
 ;; is why an event vector is legal here and rejected above.
 :passive
 {:why   "no :prefetch — the intent positions are the author's, untouched"
  :props {:to :fh.route/cart :on-mouse-enter [:fh.analytics/link-hovered]}}

 ;; The closed grammar at an OWNED intent position. Named forms only —
 ;; EDN can carry a name, and only code can carry a function or a declared
 ;; callback, so the table of names lives here and the table of values in
 ;; the suite.
 :accepted
 [{:why "a plain function — the unceremonious spelling"
   :form :bare-fn}
  {:why "a v/handler — the declared imperative form, unwrapped at the boundary"
   :form :v-handler}]

 :rejected
 [{:why "an event vector — legal at any OTHER :on-mouse-enter, and not at one the framework calls"
   :form :event-vector
   :tag  "an event vector"}
  {:why "an event options map — intent plus listener options, still intent"
   :form :event-options
   :tag  "an event options map"}
  {:why "a v/event — the declared conversion seam, and still not something to call"
   :form :v-event
   :tag  "a v/event"}]

 :error
 {:id          :rf.error/view-bad-event
  :where       v/route-link
  :recovery    :use-a-plain-fn-or-v-handler
  :legal-forms [:bare-fn :v-handler :nil]}

 ;; Substrings the human sentence must carry: the opt that narrowed the
 ;; position, the accepted form, and the event a caller reaches for when
 ;; they wanted an application reaction instead. Substring, not equality —
 ;; the sentence is prose.
 :message-mentions [":prefetch :intent" "v/handler" ":rf.route/prefetch"]

 ;; The greppable trailing token every thrown re-frame2 error carries.
 :message-token "[:rf.error/view-bad-event]"}
